From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Jun 2026 11:38:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/83] =?UTF-8?q?etc/rc.d:=20add=2016-ttdbserver=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20fixes=20CDE=20TT=5FERR=5FNO=5FMATCH?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CDE apps (dtstyle, dtcm, dtfile, ...) resolve their ToolTalk message
patterns through a type database that ttsession loads at session start.
Substrate started rpcbind (15-rpcbind) but never started rpc.ttdbserver
and never compiled the CDE ptypes, so ttsession had zero ptypes
registered: every ToolTalk request returned TT_ERR_NO_MATCH and the apps
printed "Cannot communicate with the session manager" and exited.

16-ttdbserver (sequenced after rpcbind, before 60-sdm) starts
rpc.ttdbserver and compiles /usr/dt/appconfig/tttypes/*.ptype into the
ToolTalk type DB so the types are live before dtsession starts.

The compile runs with DISPLAY unset on purpose: with DISPLAY set but no X
server up yet, tt_type_comp's ToolTalk init blocks ~20s per file trying to
reach the X-display session before falling back (this is the "tt_type_comp
hang" — it always completes, just slowly).  With DISPLAY unset it uses the
host session and compiles all ptypes in ~3s.

Verified headless: with this rc.d the type names compile correctly (the
%C/%S printf fix makes them legible) and dtsession/dtwm/ttsession come up
with a clean errorlog — no more NO_MATCH / "cannot communicate".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver | 63 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 63 insertions(+)
 create mode 100755 etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver

diff --git a/etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver b/etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..ed1142ad
--- /dev/null
+++ b/etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# /etc/rc.d/16-ttdbserver — CDE ToolTalk database server + type database.
+#
+# ToolTalk clients (dtsession, dtfile, dtstyle, dtcm, ...) resolve their
+# message patterns ("ptypes") through a type database served by
+# rpc.ttdbserver and loaded by ttsession.  With neither the server running
+# NOR the CDE ptypes compiled, every ToolTalk request fails with
+# TT_ERR_NO_MATCH and the apps print "Cannot communicate with the session
+# manager" and exit.
+#
+# Runs after 15-rpcbind (rpc.ttdbserver registers its RPC service with the
+# portmapper) and before 60-sdm (so the types are loaded by the time
+# dtsession starts ttsession).
+#
+# NOTE on the compile: tt_type_comp must run with DISPLAY UNSET.  With
+# DISPLAY set but no X server up yet, its ToolTalk init blocks ~20s per
+# file trying to reach the X-display session before falling back — so
+# compiling all ptypes at boot would otherwise add minutes of dead time.
+# With DISPLAY unset it uses the host session and compiles all of them in
+# a few seconds.
+
+TTDB=/usr/dt/bin/rpc.ttdbserver
+TTC=/usr/dt/bin/tt_type_comp
+PIDFILE=/var/run/ttdbserver.pid
+TTPATH=/root/.tt
+
+case "${1:-start}" in
+    start)
+        if [ -x "$TTDB" ]; then
+            mkdir -p /var/run
+            "$TTDB" > /var/log/ttdbserver.log 2>&1 &
+            echo $! > "$PIDFILE"
+        fi
+        # Compile the CDE ptypes into the ToolTalk type DB.  tt_type_comp -m
+        # merges idempotently, so re-running each boot is cheap (a few
+        # seconds total) and avoids depending on the DB's on-disk path.
+        if [ -x "$TTC" ]; then
+            ( unset DISPLAY
+              export HOME=/root TTPATH="$TTPATH"
+              mkdir -p "$TTPATH"
+              for f in /usr/dt/appconfig/tttypes/*.ptype; do
+                  # dtinfo*.ptype use #include and don't compile; skip them.
+                  case "$f" in *dtinfo*) continue ;; esac
+                  "$TTC" -sd user -m "$f" > /dev/null 2>&1
+              done )
+        fi
+        ;;
+    stop)
+        kill "$(cat $PIDFILE 2>/dev/null)" 2>/dev/null
+        ;;
+    status)
+        if [ -x "$TTC" ] && ( unset DISPLAY; HOME=/root TTPATH="$TTPATH" \
+                "$TTC" -sd user -p 2>/dev/null | grep -q '^ptype' ); then
+            echo "ttdbserver: ToolTalk types loaded"
+        else
+            echo "ttdbserver: no types"
+        fi
+        ;;
+    restart)
+        $0 stop
+        $0 start
+        ;;
+esac
